### Java 7: abstract methods only

```java
public interface AccountService {
    BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId);  // abstract (implicitly)
    void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount);  // abstract
}

// Every class MUST implement ALL methods
public class BasicAccountService implements AccountService {
    @Override public BigDecimal getBalance(String id) { ... }
    @Override public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) { ... }
}
```

### Java 8: default and static methods

```java
public interface AccountService {
    BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId);
    void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount);

    // Default method: has a body, implementations inherit it
    default boolean hasPositiveBalance(String accountId) {
        return getBalance(accountId).signum() > 0;
    }

    // Static method: utility, called on the interface
    static String formatAccountId(String raw) {
        return raw.trim().toUpperCase();
    }
}

// BasicAccountService gets hasPositiveBalance for free – no code change needed
String formatted = AccountService.formatAccountId(" sav2001 ");  // "SAV2001"
```

Default methods solved the backward-compatibility problem: adding `hasPositiveBalance` to the interface does not break existing implementations.

### Java 9: private methods

```java
public interface AccountService {
    default boolean isEligibleForLoan(String accountId) {
        return hasMinBalance(accountId) && hasGoodHistory(accountId);
    }

    default boolean isEligibleForOverdraft(String accountId) {
        return hasMinBalance(accountId);  // reuses the private method
    }

    // Private: code reuse within the interface, not visible to implementors
    private boolean hasMinBalance(String accountId) {
        return getBalance(accountId).compareTo(new BigDecimal("10000")) >= 0;
    }

    private boolean hasGoodHistory(String accountId) {
        return true; // simplified
    }

    BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId);
}
```

Private methods avoid duplicating code between default methods.

### Java 17: sealed interfaces

```java
public sealed interface Account permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, LoanAccount {
    BigDecimal getBalance();
}

// Only these three can implement Account
public final class SavingsAccount implements Account { ... }
public final class CurrentAccount implements Account { ... }
public final class LoanAccount implements Account { ... }

// public class FraudAccount implements Account { }  // COMPILE ERROR: not permitted
```

Sealed interfaces enable exhaustive pattern matching in switch.

### Summary table

| Feature | Java 7 | Java 8 | Java 9 | Java 17 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Abstract methods | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Constants (static final) | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| default methods | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| static methods | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| private methods | No | No | Yes | Yes |
| sealed | No | No | No | Yes |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Diamond problem:** If a class implements two interfaces with the same default method, it must override the method to resolve the conflict.
- **Default methods do not break single responsibility.** Use them for convenience methods that compose abstract methods, not for core logic.
- **Spring uses default methods** in repositories: `CrudRepository` has default save/findAll methods that `SimpleJpaRepository` overrides.
